course: medicine
lesson: 60
title: Ill on match day: play or stay home?
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Many coaches use the «above the neck» check: a runny nose or a slightly sore throat with no fever may allow gentle play if you feel well, but a chesty cough, aching body, tummy bug or fever are «below the neck» signs and mean no sport today.
explain: Ruairi has a sniffle, «It's only a sniffle, Ma, I'm grand», and his mam checks: no fever, no cough, no aches, so he can try, as long as he stops if he feels worse.
explain: Charlie at 38.5°C wants to «sweat it out», but a fever means the body is already fighting an infection and the heart is already working harder, so exercise can't cure it and doctors say rest.
explain: Hiding a fever with medicine, playing through chickenpox in long sleeves, or rushing back at full speed after flu just turns one ill player into a longer illness, and often into several ill teammates.
explain: Two cases are never about toughness: with asthma and a cold, keep the reliever inhaler close and tell the coach; with a headache and sickness after a hard knock to the head, no play and a doctor's check.
fact: The «above the neck» idea: with only a runny nose or slightly sore throat and no fever, light play may be OK if you feel well; stop if you feel worse.
fact: A chesty cough, body aches or a tummy bug are «below the neck» signs, and hard exercise then makes you sicker and slower to recover.
fact: Normal body temperature is about 37°C, and 38°C or more is a fever; doctors say no sport with a fever.
fact: Exercising with some viral infections can, rarely, inflame the heart, which is one reason for the no-sport-with-fever rule.
fact: Fever medicine like paracetamol lowers the temperature but does not remove the infection, so a hidden fever means playing ill and spreading it.
fact: The NHS says stay off school until 48 hours after the last time you were sick or had diarrhoea.
fact: The NHS says children with chickenpox stay away from school until all the spots have crusted over, usually about 5 days after they appear.
fact: Chickenpox spreads through the air as well as by touch, so covering the spots does not stop it.
fact: An ill player can pass the bug to teammates, so more players are off the next week.
fact: Fitness drops during illness; a common coaches' rule is to come back gently and build up over a few sessions.
fact: Colds often trigger asthma, so a child with asthma and a cold keeps the reliever inhaler close and tells the coach.
fact: Headache or feeling sick after a knock to the head can mean concussion, and the UK rule is «if in doubt, sit them out».
fact: «Sweet as» is New Zealand slang for fine, and «fam» is London slang for mate.
exercise: Ruairi has a runny nose, no fever, no cough, no aches. What's sensible? | He can try gentle play and stop if worse | He must stay in bed all week | He must play twice as hard | He should take fever medicine first
exercise: Amelia has a chesty cough and aching legs. Cross-country? | No, those are below-the-neck signs | Yes, pushing through toughens you | Yes, if she runs slowly at the back | Yes, coughs clear when you run
exercise: Charlie's temperature is 38.5°C. How far above normal is that? | 1.5 degrees | 0.5 degrees | 3.5 degrees | 15 degrees
exercise: Can Charlie «sweat out» his fever at training? | No, fever means no sport | Yes, sweating cures a fever | Yes, if he drinks lots | Only if he trains alone
exercise: Why do doctors say no sport with a fever? | Some viruses can rarely inflame the heart | Fever makes your legs too long | Coaches hate sweaty players | Fever makes you run too fast
exercise: Jake hides his fever with medicine before the cup game. What did the medicine do? | It hid the sign, not the illness | It cured the infection completely | It made him fitter | It stopped him spreading germs
exercise: Mia was last sick Friday 6 pm. When does the 48-hour wait end? | Sunday 6 pm | Saturday 6 am | Saturday 6 pm | Monday 6 pm
exercise: Arlo has chickenpox: «Can't I just wear long sleeves?» Why not? | Chickenpox also spreads through the air | Sleeves make spots worse | Long sleeves are banned | Only legs spread chickenpox
exercise: How long do chickenpox spots usually take to crust over? | About 5 days | About 5 hours | About 5 weeks | About 50 days
exercise: Kiara has aches and a temperature, and the team has only 10 players. What's best? | Stay home so she doesn't spread it | Play, the team needs eleven | Play in goal so she runs less and rests | Hide the temperature and play
exercise: Kiara plays ill and gives it to 3 teammates. How many are off next week? | 4 | 3 | 1 | 10
exercise: Ollie had flu for a week and gasps after ten minutes. Why? | Fitness drops while you're ill | Flu makes you fitter but more tired | He played too slowly at the start | The pitch felt longer than last week
exercise: How should Ollie come back after flu? | Short and easy first, full games later | A full match on day one | Double training to catch up | Never play again
exercise: Sanjay has asthma and a cold. What's sensible? | Keep his inhaler close and tell the coach | Leave the inhaler at home | Run extra hard to clear his chest | Hide the cold from everyone
exercise: Tom has a headache and feels sick after a hard header yesterday. Play the final? | No, it could be concussion | Yes, if he feels brave enough to play | Yes, but no heading the ball this time | Only for the second half, to be safe
exercise: Which is an «above the neck» sign? | A runny nose | Aching legs | A fever over 38°C | A tummy bug
test: Mia from Christchurch: «I'm sweet as now!» the morning after being sick. What's the rule? | Wait 48 hours after the last sick | Play as soon as you feel OK, even that day | Wait just 4 hours | Play but drink nothing
test: Amelia's friend: «Pushing through illness makes you tougher». What would actually happen? | She'd likely stay ill for longer | She'd get over it within an hour of running | She'd run her best time | Her cough would vanish
test: Jake's mate: «Once the fever's gone with medicine, you're fit». What's the flaw? | The infection is still there | The medicine makes you stronger | The fever was never real | Medicine lasts all week
test: What would happen if Jake had stayed home on match day? | He'd likely recover faster and not spread it | He'd be ill for longer | The whole team would get ill | Nothing would be different
test: Kiara's coach texts «We need you!». What's the smartest reply? | I'm ill, I'd only spread it | I'll come and hide it | I'll play in a scarf | I'll take medicine and come
test: Sanjay: «Fam, it's just a cold, I'll be fine without my inhaler». Why is that risky? | Colds often trigger asthma attacks | Colds cure asthma for a few days at a time | Inhalers make colds worse and last longer | Asthma stops in winter when the air is cold
test: Ollie: «I'm better, so I'm 100%, yeah?» Coach's answer? | Better isn't the same as back to full fitness | Better means fitter than before, because flu trains you | Flu makes muscles stronger | He should run a marathon first
test: Tom: «I'm fine, Dad, it's the final!» Why is Dad right to say no? | Headache and sickness after a knock can be concussion | Finals are bad for headaches | Tom is too young for finals | Headers are always against the rules
test: A cartoon hero with spots all over says «just a rash» and plays on. What would really happen with chickenpox? | Lots of teammates could catch it | Nobody can catch it outdoors | It clears by running | Spots fall off when you sweat hard enough
test: Ruairi's sniffle gets worse at training and he feels shaky. What now? | Stop and rest | Sprint to shake it off | Ask for a full match | Take fever medicine and continue
test: Which one means definitely no sport today? | A fever of 38°C | A slightly runny nose | Feeling a little nervous | A small graze on the knee
exam: Identify the temperature at or above which a child is considered to have a fever. | Thirty-eight degrees Celsius | Thirty-five and a half degrees Celsius | Thirty-six degrees Celsius | Forty-five degrees Celsius
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the effect of fever-reducing medication taken prior to sport? | It conceals the sign whilst the infection persists | It eradicates the infection entirely | It renders participation entirely safe | It prevents transmission to teammates
exam: It is given that a child last vomited at six o'clock on Friday evening. Determine the earliest time at which the forty-eight-hour period expires. | Six o'clock on Sunday evening | Six o'clock on Saturday morning | Six o'clock on Saturday evening | Six o'clock on Monday evening
exam: Select the response consistent with the established guidance concerning a return to sport following influenza. | A gradual return over several sessions | An immediate return to full competition | Double training to recover lost fitness | Permanent withdrawal from sport
